\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/paper/fig_teaser.pdf}
\caption{Two-axis framework for VLA token compressibility. Pruning robustness depends on both connector coupling (token-independent vs.\ token-entangling) and task complexity (single-step vs.\ multi-step). X-VLA loses 1.4\,pp on Object at $k{=}0.9$ but 27.8\,pp on Goal (random pruning), a $20{\times}$ task-complexity amplification.}
\label{fig:teaser}
\end{figure*}

Remove 5\% of vision tokens from SmolVLA and its success rate collapses from 59.2\% to 0.0\% across all task suites and pruning methods. Apply the same 5\% reduction to $\pi_0.5$ and it retains 98.2\% under L2 norm selection. Both are Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models built on a Vision Transformer (ViT) encoder feeding tokens through a connector into a language model backbone; the critical difference is not model scale (450M vs.\ 4B parameters) but the connector architecture. Token pruning is well-established for ViTs~\cite{rao2021dynamicvit,bolya2023tome,liang2022evit} and a growing body of work extends it to VLAs~\cite{cheng2026vlaiap,xu2025vlacache,yang2025efficientvla,liu2025vlapruner}, but each method validates on a single architecture.

These methods implicitly assume that pruning transfers across VLA designs. Yet current VLAs employ fundamentally different vision connectors---linear projections~\cite{black2024pi0}, MLP projectors~\cite{kim2024openvla}, pixel-shuffle folding~\cite{shukor2025smolvla}, and learned encoders with cross-token self-attention~\cite{zheng2025xvla}---each imposing distinct constraints on how visual information distributes across tokens. Deploying a pruning method validated on one connector type to another risks silent degradation or complete failure. No prior work evaluates pruning robustness across both connector architectures and task complexity.

We propose a two-axis analysis of VLA token compressibility (Figure~\ref{fig:teaser}). Along the coupling axis, token-independent connectors (linear projections, MLP projectors) degrade gracefully, while token-entangling connectors produce qualitatively different failures: SmolVLA's pixel-shuffle collapses to 0.0\% at every retention ratio, while X-VLA's BART encoder exhibits sharp threshold drops. Along the task complexity axis, under random pruning at $k{=}0.9$, X-VLA loses just 1.4 percentage points on single-step Object but 27.8 percentage points on multi-step Goal, a $20{\times}$ amplification driven entirely by task complexity. The coefficient of variation (CV) of post-connector token L2 norms captures connector coupling on a continuous spectrum (0.038 to 0.314) and correlates with both pruning tolerance and method sensitivity: at $k{=}0.95$, $\pi_0.5$ (CV$\,{=}\,$0.055) exhibits a $15{\times}$ greater method gap than X-VLA (CV$\,{=}\,$0.151).

For pixel-shuffle connectors where all pruning strategies yield 0.0\%, we introduce Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR), a lightweight cross-attention module (${\sim}$22M parameters, ${\sim}$5\% of SmolVLA) that recovers over 60\% success rate at 75\% compression through learned re-encoding.

Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item A two-axis compressibility analysis across five VLA models (450M to 7B) on four LIBERO~\cite{liu2023libero} task suites, revealing connector architecture and task complexity as interacting determinants of pruning robustness.
    \item CV of token L2 norms as a single-pass diagnostic that correlates with pruning tolerance and method sensitivity, enabling connector-appropriate compression strategy selection without downstream evaluation.
    \item Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR), which recovers a pixel-shuffle VLA from 0.0\% to over 60\% success rate at 75\% compression, where all hard pruning methods fail.
\end{itemize}
